"ei mi penmi da" wasn't true. The meeting was scheduled from 18 to 22 so th=
at=20
people going there from work could get there late.

> The only problem with "ca li ..." is that in some cases it may not be
> clear if you are giving a time or a date.

I normally say a date as "le <namcu> moi be le <masti>". If the date is sai=
d=20
in numbers and includes the year, the only time it can be mistaken for a ti=
me=20
is before the year 24, which can be remedied by saying "nono" at the=20
beginning of the year. If it's just month and day, all valid dates are also=
=20
valid times.

Another option is to use the 12-hour clock: "li <pa .. pare> pe lo=20
cerni/donri/vanci/nicte".

